[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  monday july 25th i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  last week u.s law enforcement announced  it had disrupted a north korean  state-sponsored hacking campaign that  targeted hospitals and other medical  facilities here in the u.s  the justice department said it was able  to claw back half a million dollars  worth of cryptocurrency for a kansas  medical facility that was targeted by  the hackers and identify a new type of  malware that was used  it's part of a new strategy by the biden  administration to stop state-sponsored  hackers we should note north korea has  denied it targets other countries with  cyber attacks and the north korean  mission to the united nations didn't  respond to a request for comment  for more on this specific attack and the  government's changing approach to  defending against cyber attacks we're  joined by our cyber security reporter  dustin volts hey dustin thanks for being  here thanks so much for having me  okay so let's start with why north korea  would support this kind of ransomware  attack so north korea has been a major  cyber security threat for many years  against the us and other western nations  really you know anybody in the world  that they think they can hack and and  make a buck from so you know whereas  russia and china and iran maybe have you  know sort of more uh geopolitical or  espionage oriented goals with their  hacking north korea for a number of  years now has shown that they  are desperate to make money through  their hacking and the reason for that is  because of course they are under an  enormous set of sanctions  due to their nuclear weapons program  that they continue to develop and so  hacking whether it's you know crypto  mining whether it's ransomware attacks  all sorts of different manners of sort  of malicious cyber activity it is a way  for them to evade the sanctions that  have been levied against them for a  number of years and continue to support  the regime and in some cases fund  directly their nuclear weapons program  how are authorities in this case able to  track and stop this attack  so this was yet another example of  authorities at the justice department in  the fbi working to you know follow the  money essentially but follow the money  on the blockchain and you know this was  another case where cryptocurrency was  used to pay the hackers and you know  there's sort of this mindset that i  think has been pervasive throughout the  digital currency community for a number  of years that basically all payments are  anonymous you can't really track people  but in fact the blockchain by nature  records every single transaction and  despite the you know often best efforts  of the bad guys law enforcement has  shown by partnering with a number of  cyber security firms that specialize in  cryptocurrency tracking that they can in  fact sort of find  and identify  who is getting paid the ransoms the fbi  and other agencies have shown several  times now over the past many months that  they they can use a variety of forensic  investigative techniques to sometimes  track these guys down some of those  techniques to be honest are not always  fully clear in court records or not  clearly stated exactly how they did it  because you know you can imagine that  there are certain tools that the u.s  intelligence agencies are using to sort  of help out in investigating some of  these hacks but you know the capacity is  there for  investigators to  follow the money on the blockchain and  in some cases work to  seize funds that are left in you know a  digital currency wallet in this case  that's what happened here the heck of  the kansas medical facility brought in  help from law enforcement and they were  able to claw back about a half a million  dollars and what's not clear is you know  how much was not clawed back it's not  totally clear that this was the entirety  of the payment in this specific hack and  it's also not clear  you know how much money the north korean  hackers were able to ransom in other  cases and still have access to  right and while i'm sure the kansas  facility is is pleased to have its money  returned to it at least some of it there  is this bigger question of what happens  to the people behind the attack you know  if there's from north korea if it's a  state-sponsored attack how would the u.s  hold these people accountable and i  guess that goes for other foreign  hackers as well does the us have to  change its strategy when it comes to  tracking these people down so this is  always a problem when we talk about  prosecuting hacking cases particularly  nation state cases where  you know you're talking about people in  saint petersburg or tehran or pyongyang  who are maybe never going to travel the  united states and are not going to face  an american courtroom this is not a new  phenomenon you know occasionally some of  these hackers might travel or vacation  to you know some island somewhere or you  know the coast of spain and then local  authorities will nab them and then turn  them over to the united states and so  there's always a possibility of that but  really you know i think there's a  recognition in the biodemonstration and  certainly in this justice department  under deputy attorney lisa monaco who  announced the results of their case here  and the seizure of the money with the  kansas medical facility there's a  recognition that that doesn't doesn't  really cut it and so you know a big part  of the strategy that miss monaco has  articulated over you know weeks and  months now is that we want to basically  do what we did for counter-terrorism  after september 11th which is you know  thwart the bad activity before it  happens or if it is happening to  diminish the overall  amount of damage that can be done  has the justice department or deputy  attorney general lisa monaco explained  what they mean by they're treating it  like fighting terrorism because you know  you don't necessarily know about a cyber  attack until it's happening and apart  from telling companies to boost their  cyber security is there anything  authorities can do ahead of these  attacks there are things that you know  the nsa and and u.s cyber command  working in tandem again with the fbi and  others  there are things that they do where they  are you know able to sort of get an idea  of what  hackers whether they're in north korea  or somewhere else what they intend to do  before they do it and you know there's  something that general paul nakasoni who  leads the national security agency talks  about all the time which is defend ford  and it's really the idea of  using offensive capabilities of you know  surveillance uh hacking into networks of  the bad guys abroad and using  intelligence gleaned from that to  understand you know what they're doing  who they're harming and what they might  do next  and increasingly you're sort of seeing  a push to use that intelligence not to  you know understand what's going on but  to you know sort of prevent the next  attack to minimize the next attack to  educate the public and businesses and so  forth about you know specific threats  that they should be vigilant about and  steps that they can take to potentially  minimize it in addition to in some cases  maybe  taking servers offline that the hackers  are using especially if they  are you know located in a place where we  might have an allied government that  wants to help us with that  all right that's our reporter dustin  volts thanks so much for joining us  again dustin thank you and that's it for  today's tech news briefing if you want  more tech stories check out our website  wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

